//
// Regression: the XML writer discarded a whole profile over one bad tag (#1779).
//
// Reported as "XML Writer Unable to output tag with type swpt | Json Ok": on a
// fuzzed profile iccToXml printed that line and wrote no file at all, while
// iccToJson on the same input produced a document. The 'swpt' in the message is
// incidental -- it is simply the first tag whose serialize fails, not a type the
// XML side lacks a handler for.
//
// The real difference was the writers' failure policy. When a tag's serialize
// returned false the JSON writer did `continue` and kept the rest of the
// document (IccProfileJson.cpp), while the XML writer did `return false` and
// abandoned the entire profile (IccProfileXml.cpp, and the struct-member loop in
// IccTagXml.cpp). One unserializable tag therefore cost every other tag too.
//
// The fix brings XML to parity at the two levels where a container is keyed by
// name and an omission is representable: top-level tags and struct members are
// skipped, each leaving an XML comment recording what was dropped. The two other
// emit points are deliberately left strict -- array elements are positional and
// the XML array format cannot express a hole (CIccTagXmlArray::ParseXml sizes the
// array by counting element children and then rejects any unfilled slot), and
// CIccMpeXmlTintArray's array is what defines the element, which is also where
// the JSON writer returns false. Both of those now merely fail their own tag,
// which the profile-level skip absorbs, so the document still gets written.
//
// Validating that new output exposed a second, pre-existing defect this test also
// pins: CIccTagXmlStruct/CIccTagXmlArray emitted "<privateStruct .../>" and
// "<privateArray .../>" self-closed while still writing "</privateStruct>" /
// "</privateArray>" at the end, so any profile with an unregistered struct or
// array signature serialized to XML that no parser accepts. It was invisible
// while such documents were being thrown away wholesale.
//
// Both PoCs are driven the way the icc_toxml fuzzer and the IccToXml CLI drive
// them -- Read() from an in-memory buffer into a CIccProfileXml, then ToXml() --
// and the output is parsed back with libxml2 to assert it is well-formed, which
// is the assertion that fails if either fix is reverted.
//
// Exit code 0 = pass, 1 = a case regressed.
#include "IccProfileXml.h"
#include "IccTagXmlFactory.h"
#include "IccMpeXmlFactory.h"
#include "IccIO.h"

#include <libxml/parser.h>
#include <libxml/tree.h>

#include <cstdio>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

